item.description:
Score 21 | Comments 5 (<a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=30329279">thread link</a>) | @arraypad
<br/>
February 13, 2022 | https://repography.com/blog/go-first-commit | <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/*/https://repography.com/blog/go-first-commit">archive.org</a>
<br/><!-- hnss:readable-content --><hr/><div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><p>While working on Repography I've explored a ton of open source Git repos. Recently looking at <a href="https://github.com/golang/go">golang/go</a>, I was amazed to see the first commit appears to be from computing legend Brian Kernighan in July 1972.</p><figure><pre><code><pre><code>commit 7d7c6a97f815e9279d08cfaea7d5efb5e90695a8
Author: Brian Kernighan &lt;bwk&gt;
Date:   Tue Jul 18 19:05:45 1972 -0500

    hello, world

    R=ken
    DELTA=7  (7 added, 0 deleted, 0 changed)

diff --git a/src/pkg/debug/macho/testdata/hello.b b/src/pkg/debug/macho/testdata/hello.b
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..05c4140424
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/pkg/debug/macho/testdata/hello.b
@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
+main( ) {
+       extrn a, b, c;
+       putchar(a); putchar(b); putchar(c); putchar('!*n');
+} 
+a 'hell';
+b 'o, w';
+c 'orld';
</code></pre></code></pre><figcaption><a href="https://github.com/golang/go/commit/7d7c6a97f815e9279d08cfaea7d5efb5e90695a8" target="_blank">The very first commit in golang/go</a>. Presumably submitted via fax.</figcaption></figure><p>Brian is then hard at work for the next few years:</p><ul><li>Sun Jan 20 01:02:03 1974: <a href="https://github.com/golang/go/commit/0bb0b61d6a85b2a1a33dcbc418089656f2754d32" target="_blank">convert to C</a></li><li>Fri Apr 1 02:02:04 1988: <a href="https://github.com/golang/go/commit/0744ac969119db8a0ad3253951d375eb77cfce9e" target="_blank">convert to Draft-Proposed ANSI C</a></li><li>Fri Apr 1 02:03:04 1988: <a href="https://github.com/golang/go/commit/d82b11e4a46307f1f1415024f33263e819c222b8" target="_blank">last-minute fix: convert to ANSI C</a></li></ul><p>In 1988 the team decided to take a break <!-- -->—<!-- --> for about 20 years <!-- -->—<!-- --> before Robert Griesemer's first commit, <a href="https://github.com/golang/go/commit/18c5b488a3b2e218c0e0cf2a7d4820d9da93a554" target="_blank">Go spec starting point</a>.</p><p>It's interesting to note that the file from these early commits (the final ANSI C version) is <a href="https://github.com/golang/go/blob/master/src/debug/macho/testdata/hello.c" target="_blank">still in the Go repo today</a>.</p><p>I absolutely love that someone took the time to implant this easter egg and that it still endures, in such a prominent project.</p><figure><video controls="" width="1600" height="1148" poster="https://assets.repography.com/file/repography-assets/blog/go-first-commit/repography-go.jpg"><source src="https://assets.repography.com/file/repography-assets/blog/go-first-commit/repography-go.webm" type="video/webm"><source src="https://assets.repography.com/file/repography-assets/blog/go-first-commit/repography-go.mp4" type="video/mp4">Sorry, your browser doesn't appear to support embedded videos.</video><figcaption>The real history through the lens of our Time and Influence poster</figcaption></figure><p>Finally, having fiddled with the time range a bit, I rendered the poster I intended to... I think this makes it super clear how the core team has evolved over time. It's also encouraging that several of the top contributors appear to have become involved relatively recently.</p><figure><p><img src="https://assets.repography.com/file/repography-assets/blog/go-first-commit/go-bubbles.png" alt="The top contributors to golang/go, from 1972 (well, 2008) to 2022"></p><figcaption>The top contributors to golang/go, from 1972 (well, 2008) to 2022</figcaption></figure><p>I wonder what other easter eggs and interesting stories are hiding in commit histories? If you have any suggestions please <a href="https://repography.com/contact">let us know</a>.</p></div></div>

Score 18 | Comments 9 (<a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=30320114">thread link</a>) | @chauhankiran
<br/>
February 13, 2022 | https://chauhankiran.github.io/vala2.html | <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/*/https://chauhankiran.github.io/vala2.html">archive.org</a>
<br/><!-- hnss:readable-content --><hr/><div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
      <p>We'll continue our journey with writing the hello, world program in Vala but this time with GTK(4) library.</p>

      <p>Let's create a new folder with name <code>gtk</code> and go inside.</p>

      <pre><code>$ mkdir gtk
$ cd gtk</code></pre>

      <p>
        Let's create two new files with name <code>Hello.vala</code> and <code>main.vala</code>. Yes, the plan is to
        continue from where we end in the <a href="https://chauhankiran.github.io/vala1.html">previous article</a> and then slowly build the working
        version of hello, world using GTK.
      </p>

      <pre><code>$ touch Hello.vala
$ touch main.vala</code></pre>

      <p>
        Open this newly created <code>main.vala</code> and <s>copy</s> write the same code we wrote previously in
        <code>hello/main.vala</code> file. In other words, <code>main()</code> method.
      </p>

      <pre><code>int main(string[] args) {
  string name = 'world';

  var hello = new Hello(name);
  hello.greet();

  return 0;
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Next, open that newly created <code>Hello.vala</code> and <s>copy</s> write the same code we wrote previously in
        <code>hello/Hello.vala</code> file. In other words, <code>Hello</code> class.
      </p>

      <pre><code>public class Hello {
  public string name;

  public Hello(string name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  public void greet() {
    stdout.printf("hello, %s\n", this.name);
  }
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Let's extend this <code>Hello</code> class by
        <a href="https://valadoc.org/gtk4/Gtk.Application.html"><code>Gtk.Application</code></a> class as we're now
        creating the GTK application! This <em>extending</em> is done via <code>:</code> operator.
      </p>

      <pre data-line="1"><code>public class Hello : Gtk.Application {
  public string name;

  public Hello(string name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  public void greet() {
    stdout.printf("hello, %s\n", this.name);
  }
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        We do not needed this <code>greet()</code> method anymore as we're interested in displaying the hello, world in
        window (or in application) and not in terminal!
      </p>

      <pre><code>public class Hello : Gtk.Application {
  public string name;

  public Hello(string name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
}
</code></pre>

      <p>
        We created the <code>name</code> variable specially to print the value of it in <code>greet()</code> method. As
        we do not have <code>greet()</code> method now, I don't think we now need any code that is associated with
        <code>name</code> variable. But, yes, we surely need that public method. So, let's have the empty public method
        for further usage.
      </p>

      <pre data-line="2-4"><code>public class Hello : Gtk.Application {
  public Hello() {

  }
}
</code></pre>

      <p>
        As per documentation, we need to call the <code>Object()</code> method from this public method. This
        <code>Object()</code> method accepts two arguments - <code>application_id</code>, unique
        <a href="https://wiki.gnome.org/HowDoI/ChooseApplicationID">application id</a> in reverse domain name notation
        or RDNS and <a href="https://valadoc.org/gio-2.0/GLib.ApplicationFlags.html"><code>flags</code></a> with one of
        the predefined values and
        <a href="https://valadoc.org/gio-2.0/GLib.ApplicationFlags.FLAGS_NONE.html"><code>ApplicationFlags.FLAGS_NONE</code></a>
        value in our case. All in all, you need to type something like this. If you have GitHub/GitLab account, change
        the value of <code>application_id</code> based on your preference.
      </p>

      <pre data-line="3-6"><code>public class Hello : Gtk.Application {
  public Hello() {
    Object(
      application_id: "com.github.chauhankiran.repo",
      flags: ApplicationFlags.FLAGS_NONE
    );
  }
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        We'll talk about this <code>application_id</code> and <code>flags</code> in future. For now, you need to type
        couple of things for GTK to properly initialize and run the application.
        <em>It's like <a href="https://wiki.c2.com/?HollywoodPrinciple">framework</a>, write this and that for us to call
          properly!</em>
      </p>

      <p>Next, we need to implement the <code>activate()</code> method that return nothing e.g. <code>void</code>.</p>

      <pre data-line="9-11"><code>public class Hello : Gtk.Application {
  public Hello() {
    Object(
      application_id: "com.github.chauhankiran.repo",
      flags: ApplicationFlags.FLAGS_NONE
    );
  }

  void activate() {

  }
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        This <code>activate()</code> method already existing in <code>Gtk.Application</code> and what we actually need
        to do is, to <em>override</em> using <code>override</code> keyword.
      </p>

      <pre data-line="9"><code>public class Hello : Gtk.Application {
  public Hello() {
    Object(
      application_id: "com.github.chauhankiran.repo",
      flags: ApplicationFlags.FLAGS_NONE
    );
  }

  override void activate() {

  }
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        I assume you don't forget the rule about access modifiers. In this <code>activate()</code> method case, we'll go
        with <code>protected</code> access modifier as that's what we ask for us!
      </p>

      <pre data-line="9"><code>public class Hello : Gtk.Application {
  public Hello() {
    Object(
      application_id: "com.github.chauhankiran.repo",
      flags: ApplicationFlags.FLAGS_NONE
    );
  }

  protected override void activate() {

  }
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        And this method <em>activates</em> the application. Again, it might possible that you get confused with this
        code. But, this is something you need to type as its like framework (I know library, whatever)!
      </p>

      <p>
        If the application is activated, we need to do something. How about displaying an application widow? That's what
        you're suppose to do with desktop application, right!
      </p>

      <p>
        Within this <code>activate()</code> method, let's create a window. In GTK, <code>window</code> is
        <em>widget</em>. I mean everything that is a graphical component is widget including menu, button, label,
        checkbox, list, and so on. These all are widgets in GTK and GTK has separate class(es) for each of these
        widgets!
      </p>

      <p>
        Let's create a <code>window</code> widget using
        <a href="https://valadoc.org/gtk4/Gtk.ApplicationWindow.html"><code>ApplicationWindow</code></a> class. This
        <code>ApplicationWindow</code> class accepts the
        <a href="https://valadoc.org/gtk4/Gtk.ApplicationWindow.ApplicationWindow.html">application</a> as the argument
        which is <code>this</code> or class itself as that is the application (remember, we extend the
        <code>Gtk.Application</code> to make <code>Hello</code> as application).
      </p>

      <pre data-line="10"><code>public class Hello : Gtk.Application {
  public Hello() {
    Object(
      application_id: "com.github.chauhankiran.repo",
      flags: ApplicationFlags.FLAGS_NONE
    );
  }

  protected override void activate() {
    var window = new Gtk.ApplicationWindow(this);
  }
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        We just created the window for the application. We now need to invoke the <code>.show()</code> method on this
        <code>window</code> object to actually display it when <code>activate()</code> method executes.
      </p>

      <pre data-line="11"><code>public class Hello : Gtk.Application {
  public Hello() {
    Object(
      application_id: "com.github.chauhankiran.repo",
      flags: ApplicationFlags.FLAGS_NONE
    );
  }

  protected override void activate() {
    var window = new Gtk.ApplicationWindow(this);
    window.show();
  }
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        This is our simple window application! Before we go further and run the application, we need to do some minor
        modifications in <code>main.vala</code> as the current code create an instance of <code>Hello</code> class and
        run the <code>greet()</code> method which is not valid anymore.
      </p>

      <p>Let's first remove the line that call the <code>greet()</code> method.</p>

      <pre><code>int main(string[] args) {
  string name = 'world';

  var hello = new Hello(name);

  return 0;
}
</code></pre>

      <p>
        We also not need <code>name</code> variable and also remove it as argument being passed while creating an
        instance.
      </p>
      <pre data-line="2"><code>int main(string[] args) {
  var hello = new Hello();

  return 0;
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        On this <code>hello</code> object, we need to call <code>.run()</code> method to actually run the application
        instance. This method accepts <code>args</code> as argument.
      </p>

      <pre data-line="3"><code>int main(string[] args) {
  var hello = new Hello();
  hello.run(args);

  return 0;
}</code></pre>

      <p>
        This <code>.run()</code> method returns an integer that we can directly return instead of returning the explicit
        <code>0</code>.
      </p>

      <pre data-line="3"><code>int main(string[] args) {
  var hello = new Hello();
  return hello.run(args);
}</code></pre>

      <p>With this change in <code>main.vala</code>, the application is now ready to be run!</p>

      <p>You know how to run the Vala programs, right?</p>

      <pre><code>$ valac Hello.vala main.vala -o Hello &amp;&amp; ./Hello
hello, world
</code></pre>

      <p>
        I copied this command from <a href="https://chauhankiran.github.io/vala1.html">previous article</a> and you know that this will print the
        <code>hello, world</code> as output based on code we wrote in those files.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is correct! But, we need to do one more modification to this command as we're now using GTK library. We
        need to inform Vala compiler to use GTK library while compiling the programs using <code>--pkg</code> flag
        followed by the library name or <code>gtk4</code> (installed name of the GTK library) in our case. Putting all
        these together, following is the command we need to run.
      </p>

      <pre data-line="1"><code>$ valac --pkg gtk4 Hello.vala main.vala -o Hello &amp;&amp; ./Hello</code></pre>

      <p><img src="https://chauhankiran.github.io/images/output-1.png" alt="Hello application"></p>

      <p>
        After running this command, you should see the default 200x200 window with title as application name e.g.
        <code>Hello</code>. But, this is not the hello, world program as we're not seeing hello, world at any place
        within the application.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let's print <code>hello, world</code> instead of <code>Hello</code> as title. This can be done via updating the
        <em>attribute</em> value of <code>window</code> widget. <code>window</code> has many attributes including
        <code>title</code> (for title of the window), <code>default_width</code> (for default width of window),
        <code>default_height</code> (for default height of window), and so on.
      </p>

      <p>
        We're now going to assign <code>hello, world</code> to <code>window</code>'s <code>title</code> and
        <code>300</code> to both <code>default_width</code> and <code>default_height</code> to create 300x300 window in
        dimension.
      </p>

      <pre data-line="11-13"><code>public class Hello : Gtk.Application {
  public Hello() {
    Object(
      application_id: "com.github.chauhankiran.repo",
      flags: ApplicationFlags.FLAGS_NONE
    );
  }

  protected override void activate() {
    var window = new Gtk.ApplicationWindow(this);
    window.title = "hello, world";
    window.default_height = 300;
    window.default_width = 300;

    window.show();
  }
}</code></pre>

      <p>Running this program should show the hello, world as title.</p>

      <pre><code>$ valac --pkg gtk4 Hello.vala main.vala -o Hello &amp;&amp; ./Hello</code></pre>

      <p><img src="https://chauhankiran.github.io/images/output-2.png" alt="hello, world application"></p>

      <p>
        This is the first version of the hello, world program using GTK library. We can go further and display the
        hello, world text within application's window.
      </p>

      <p>
        To display the text in window, we need to create a <em>label</em> widget using <code>Gtk.Label</code> class just
        like we created <code>window</code> using <code>Gtk.ApplicationWindow</code> class. While creating a
        <code>label</code> widget, we need to pass the text which is <code>hello, world</code> in our case.
      </p>

      <pre data-line="15"><code>public class …</code></pre></div></div>
<br/><p><em>Article truncated for RSS feed. Read the full article at <a href="https://chauhankiran.github.io/vala2.html">https://chauhankiran.github.io/vala2.html</a></em></p>
